ilures major  charges alleging the widespread illegal  use of insider information his firm  solomon brothers stands accused of  illegal trading in government bonds  [Music]  i was a 25 year old art history major  who knew very little about money and yet  grown-ups were paying me hundreds of  thousands of dollars a year to move huge  piles of money around i thought i was  simply documenting the end of an era of  absolute madness on wall street but it  turns out this era was just the  beginning of something bigger  of wall street grabbing an ever larger  share of the u.s economy  of the people on wall street living by  one set of rules and most everyone else  living by another  i moved on after i wrote the book i mean  i never even read it again  but i've written a couple of more books  about wall street and i know that the  world described in liar's poker has  changed wall street used to be full of  telephones with cords on them and  shouting and big hairy men it was crude  and rude and socially unacceptable wall  street's now gone quiet its most  important sound is the hum of a stack of  computer servers  the people take a lot more care and not  to draw attention to themselves  i think that's the main reason that  people still read my book why say  interns on wall street are assigned it  as homework because i'd gotten the last  glimpse of the great beast before it  ducked back inside its cave and i  accidentally wrote something that  endured just recently i found out that  the audiobook writes to liars poker had  reverted back to me so i decided to  reopen liar's poker and record it again  the whole thing ready  [Music]  it was a truly weird experience almost  like meeting a different me  it was actually difficult to read the  book again  but it reminded me of the value of not  always just moving forward of taking the  time to stop and go back  i've thrown everything about liars poker  into a couple of bankers boxes and left  them sealed up in a storage unit for  decades  after we recorded the audiobook i pulled  those boxes out and looked inside  there was a whole other me in there  a guy who for a long time couldn't even  see a decent book title staring him  right in the face  but also a guy who knew he had a good  story in front of him and i had to  admire his strange decision to leave a  huge pile of money behind to write a  book that he thought could change the  world  even if it didn't change the world in  the ways he expected  the liars poker audiobook and companion  podcast other people's money are out now  yeah look at michael lewis back in the  day i like what he said he thought it  would be a cautionary tale instead of  his instruction manual that's right  readers decide what it means we'll be  right back
 

